Z||000000||bHooper||05-21-2000||05:51 PM||||i cant get the right turn signal to blink!  i have replaced all of the bulbs and the lights stay on, but no blink blink blink!<P>any clues?  or traditional GM problems, that i should look for?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||sully5||05-21-2000||07:58 PM||||Did you try replacing the flasher?||||reg||1
Z||000002||redfiero||05-21-2000||08:20 PM||||Hoop,<BR>Had the exact same problem with my 88.  I checked everything.  Turned out that one of the wires going into light socket had come loose.  The light stayed on constantly but wouldn't blink.  Even when I looked at the socket, it wasn't noticeable that the wire was broken.  I think it was the ground wire...can't remember though.<BR>Hope this helps<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||05-21-2000||08:40 PM||||sully, what and where is the flasher?<P>refiero... will inspect for bad wires tommorow.  didnt think of that.<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-21-2000||09:12 PM||||the turn can is in the framing under the dast to the left of the steering column. the one next to the heater duct on the pasenger side is the hazard can. no you can't swap the two. hazard can might work for a test but will blink really slow.<P>Wire problems usually only effect one side. if you use the hazard can to test pull the hazard switch Before you test turn signals and see if all the lamps flash. (2 front 4 back and the front markers on a notch back. I think GT is the same.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||BroCar 87||05-21-2000||09:37 PM||||HEY   play with the bulbs a little. The same thing happened to mine and it was a good bulb. I took out the bulb, and put it in backwards. IT WORKED !  i am no expert but   just reverse the way you put in a bulb to see if that is the prob.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sully5||05-21-2000||09:44 PM||||Yep, what ogre said.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||05-21-2000||10:11 PM||||the hazards all blink!  so does that mean it is the "flasher can"... if so, can i pick one of these up at AutoZone?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000008||sully5||05-21-2000||10:38 PM||||Sounds like it could be a good possibility. You can get one just about anywhere they sell car stuff. Like ogre said, you can also swap the flasher for the hazard lights with the turn signal flasher to test. A new flasher shouldn't be more than a couple of bucks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||05-22-2000||07:39 PM||||Well guess what... the right side <B>STILL</B> doesnt flash!  I have replaced the "flasher", replaced the bulbs, and checked the sockets, and still nothing but a solid light!<P>Yet the hazards still work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> !<P>Hellllpppppppp!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>What do I need to check now?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||05-22-2000||07:52 PM||||Just a wild guess here, but it sounds like you're to the desperate measures stage any way.<P>Check the front marker bulb closely. I know it blinks with the front blinker with the lights off and opposite of it with lights on. Therefore the way it is getting power/ground changes with lights on.<P>Also, does the inside indicator work?||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||05-22-2000||09:43 PM||||I just did my first night drive, and noticed that the right turn signal indicator light stays lit while the courtesy/head lights are on!  Yet the drivers side lights continue to function completly normal!<P>hmmm???<P>As for the right side lights... DRH, they do not blink at all... just stay solid when i put the turn signal on.<P>I will recheck the front light tommorow.<P>So, does it sound like it is a bad ground?  Where is the grounding location for the turn signals?.....||||reg||1
Z||000012||DRH||05-23-2000||01:33 AM||||Ah ha...<P>The RH indicator coming on means you are getting a connection between the parking light circuit and the right turn signal. It will most likely be:<P>- bulb improperly installed in the right front or a bad socket. The bulb and socket should have 2 "connectors" on the bottom. If you used a single filament bulb the single connector on the bottom of the bulb could be connecting the 2 pins in the bottom of the socket. (kind of hard to explain, if you look at it maybe you'll see???)<P>- right side marker light dead shorted. (marker light should not be on if this is the problem)<P>- wires touching elsewhere. Could be most anywhere, from the dash to the front end, most likely in or around front socket. The wires in question are Dark Blue (R Turn) and Brown (parking).<P>Checks that stuff out and see what you can see... <P>Good Luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||05-23-2000||04:39 AM||||I had the exact same problem.. the only thing wrong was that the weatherpack in the socket wasn't seated properly.  Didn't allow the connectors to fully contact the bulb.  Pushed the weatherpack in, an presto! i have a left turn signal.||||reg||1
Z||000014||redfiero||05-23-2000||07:02 AM||||Hoop,<BR>Would bet that you have a bad ground.  Thats exactly what happened to me.  My dash indicator light stayed on also!  Tell you what you can do...you should ground the front bulb using a different piece of wire.  Do not count on the one in the socket.  Then try the turn signal.  When I did mine, I left the turn signal on and started trying to ground it.  Eventually, it blinked...and my dash indicator went out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||bHooper||05-23-2000||07:39 AM||||which one is the ground wire? and where is the ground point?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000016||sully5||05-23-2000||08:14 AM||||Hoop - sorry the flasher didn't fix your problem. Im not sure, but I think the brown wire is the ground. I'm sure one of these other guys on the forum will know for sure. As for the grounding location, I don't have a clue. I do know that if you need a new socket the Fiero Store has them, haven't checked anywhere else.||||reg||1
Z||000017||redfiero||05-23-2000||11:33 AM||||Can't recall which is the ground...if its not raining when I get home, I'll look.  The ground wire goes back into the harness somewhere.  When I did mine, the wire appeared to okay.  I stumble onto it by having my turn signal on and removing the bulb.  What part of the country are you in?||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||05-23-2000||01:23 PM||||i will have to give it a good look over this evening.  it's gotta be a ground problem, if its not the bulbs and its not the flasher... that only leaves the wireing or the colum itself!<P>i am in North Carolina.||||reg||1
Z||000019||redfiero||05-23-2000||05:36 PM||||Hoop,<BR>I took my turn signal apart.  I have a blue, brown and black wire.  The blue and the black go together into the back of the socket.  The black goes into the side(from the back) of the socket.  The black wire is the one I had trouble with.<BR>Try this...turn your signal on.  Remove the black wire.  Ground the receptacle (where the black wire was) to the closest piece of metal.  Your light should blink.  If it does, your problem is in that connection.  Good Luck.  Let me know one way or the other!||||reg||1
Z||000020||bHooper||05-23-2000||10:23 PM||||I borrowed my pal's multimeter, started at the front checking for ground.  Wouldnt you know it... the black wire fell apart at the conector!<P>One trip to Advanced Auto later, and voila!  Instant turn signals!!!!!!<P><BR>Yea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks to everyone that offered advice!  And to you, redfiero, for taking apart your turn signal and replying to me long b4 I was able to check the net and see what you learned.  Thanks!<P>Hoop||||reg||1
Z||000021||DRH||05-24-2000||12:27 PM||||Looked at the wiring diagram last night and it makes sense now. The turn signal and parking light share a common ground. The inside indicator has a seperate ground. If the black wire isn't grounded the case acts as a connection between the two filaments in the bulb. <P>So... when you turn the parking lights on the current flows thru both filaments in the bulb, back thru the indicator bulb to it's ground.<P>Good call redfiero!!!||||reg||14
